    <string name="help_title">Help</string>
    <string name="help_summary">How to add games and firmware</string>
    <string name="help_intro">RetroDroid reads a single library folder on shared storage. This page explains how games and firmware are laid out so the app can find them.</string>
    <string name="help_section_setup">Library setup</string>
    <string name="help_setup_access">Grant “All files access” when the app asks, otherwise the library cannot be read.</string>
    <string name="help_setup_structure">The folder layout is fixed: one folder per system for games, and one for the firmware that system needs.</string>
    <string name="help_setup_tree">ROMS/&lt;system&gt;/ — games\nBIOS/&lt;system&gt;/&lt;model&gt;/ — firmware</string>
    <string name="help_setup_refresh">After copying files, open the system and tap Refresh. Game counts on the home screen update when you return.</string>
    <string name="help_section_games">Adding games</string>
    <string name="help_games_intro">Copy each system’s games into its own folder. Only the extensions listed below are scanned, regardless of case; some systems also accept .zip packages.</string>
    <string name="help_formats">Formats: %1$s</string>
    <string name="help_games_subfolders">Subfolders are scanned too and appear as folders in the game list. Files whose names start with a dot are ignored.</string>
    <string name="help_games_companions">Keep companion data files in the same folder as the game. LavaX programs, for example, load their data from that folder.</string>
    <string name="help_section_firmware">Adding BIOS and firmware</string>
    <string name="help_bios_intro">Firmware belongs in BIOS/&lt;system&gt;/. Keep the exact file names and subfolders shown below — matches are case-sensitive.</string>
    <string name="help_bios_import">The quickest route is to open the system and tap Import firmware, then pick the files. You can also copy them manually; they are picked up on refresh.</string>
    <string name="help_bios_flagged">The home screen flags systems whose required firmware is missing, and the system page lists what every machine model needs.</string>
    <string name="help_optional_suffix"> (optional)</string>
    <string name="help_section_firmware_only">Firmware-only systems</string>
    <string name="help_firmware_only_body">These systems have no game content: copy the dumps of the model you want, pick that model on the system page, then tap Start firmware.</string>
    <string name="help_section_playing">Playing</string>
    <string name="help_playing_body">Tap a game to start it. Press Back for the pause menu: save or load one of five slots with previews, take a screenshot, change settings without leaving the game, reset, open core options and exit. The game is saved automatically when you leave, and the next launch offers to continue from that point. On-screen buttons mirror the original controls; dictionary systems use an on-screen keyboard.</string>
    <string name="help_section_trouble">Troubleshooting</string>
    <string name="help_trouble_game">A game does not appear — check its extension and that it sits under the right ROMS/&lt;system&gt; folder, then tap Refresh.</string>
    <string name="help_trouble_firmware">Firmware is not detected — compare file names and folder spelling letter by letter, then tap Refresh or restart the app.</string>
    <string name="help_trouble_access">Nothing is found at all — grant All files access on the home screen card.</string>
